    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Wildlife</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> The Habitat of Wildlife</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in descriptions and narratives. Interactive: Engage in discussions about personal and school experiences based on texts heard.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details from spoken narratives about animal habitats (e.g., "Which animal is faster, the jaguar or the deer?" "The deer is faster."). Can listen to descriptions of wildlife and respond to questions (e.g., "What animals live in Panama?"). Can listen to descriptions of personal and school experiences involving animals and engage in discussions by sharing opinions and asking follow-up questions (e.g., "During our school trip, we saw a sloth hanging from a tree." "What did you see during the trip?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE</h2>
<h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing pictures of different Panamanian animals (e.g., a jaguar, a sloth, a harpy eagle, a frog). Students call out the names of the animals they recognize. The teacher asks, "Where do these animals live?" to introduce the concept of 'habitat'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or images of key vocabulary: <i>habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, predator, prey, endangered, species</i>. The teacher says each word clearly, modeling pronunciation, including long and short vowel sounds (e.g., /iːg/ "eagle", /frɒg/ "frog") and syllable division for "wildlife" (wild-life). The teacher uses gestures and simple sentences: "This is a <b>jaguar</b>. It lives in the <b>forest</b>. The <b>forest</b> is its <b>habitat</b>. Jaguars are <b>predators</b>. Deer are their <b>prey</b>. Some animals, like the harpy eagle, are <b>endangered species</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a house an animal's natural habitat?" (No, a forest or jungle is).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a jaguar a predator or prey?" (A predator).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Wildlife")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (1-1.5 minutes) titled "Animals of the Panamanian Jungle." The audio includes descriptions of a jaguar, a sloth, and a harpy eagle, focusing on their habitats, diets, and key characteristics. It uses grammar such as "There are many animals...", "The jaguar is faster than the sloth...", and "The sloth moves slowly." The register is informative and descriptive, suitable for A2.2 learners. The format is a short descriptive narrative.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Identify specific information in descriptions and narratives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks, "What animals does the audio talk about?" and "What is the main topic?" (Panama's wildlife and where they live).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Specific information):</b> Students listen again and answer True/False questions based on the audio. E.g., "The sloth is faster than the jaguar." (False), "Harpy eagles live in the jungle." (True), "There are many diverse species in Panama." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Matching):</b> Students listen and match the animal to its key characteristic heard in the audio. E.g., Jaguar -> hunts prey, Sloth -> moves slowly, Harpy Eagle -> strongest bird.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details, comparative and superlative adjectives, "there is/are," present simple for habits, vocabulary: <i>habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, predator, prey, species, jungle, live, grow, hunt, survive, endangered, diverse, camouflaged</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Listen and Fill:</b> The teacher provides a handout with sentences containing blanks. Students listen to short audio clips (teacher-read or recorded) and fill in the missing target vocabulary words or comparative/superlative adjectives.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "The harpy eagle is the __________ bird in Panama." (strongest)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "There are many __________ animals in Panama." (endangered)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: "Jaguars __________ in the forest." (live)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice - Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher plays audio clips describing two animals, then asks a comparative question. Students listen and choose the correct answer from two options. The teacher emphasizes pronunciation of long/short vowel sounds in words like "snake" vs. "frog."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio: "The sloth moves very slowly. The monkey moves quickly." Question: "Which animal is __________?" (faster/slower). Students choose and say "The monkey is faster."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio: "This is a tiny frog. This is a big snake." Question: "Which animal is __________?" (smaller/larger). Students choose and say "The frog is smaller."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Listen and Share:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays a short audio narrative about a fictional school trip to a Panamanian wildlife reserve, mentioning sights like "a sloth hanging from a tree" or "observing diverse species." After listening, pairs discuss what they heard for 1-2 minutes, using phrases like "What did you hear about the sloth?" and "During their trip, they saw..." This practices the sociolinguistic competence of facilitating group discussions and discussing personal/school experiences.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Panama's Wildlife")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Panama Wildlife Listening Challenge." Students work in small groups of 3-4. The teacher plays several short audio descriptions (20-30 seconds each) of different Panamanian animals and their habitats. Each description includes facts about their habits, characteristics, or comparisons. After each audio clip, groups listen and then briefly discuss and write down:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The animal described.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One specific fact they heard (e.g., its habitat, if it's nocturnal, or a comparative aspect).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Audio 1:</b> "This animal is a large predator that hunts at night. It is stronger than most other animals in the jungle. There are not many of these left, so they are endangered. It lives in the deep forest here in Panama."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Group discussion prompt:</b> "What animal is this? What did you hear about its habitat or characteristic?" Students use target vocabulary (<i>predator, jungle, forest, endangered</i>) and grammar (<i>stronger than, there are, lives in</i>) to discuss and formulate their answers. This task encourages collaborative listening and oral reporting based on heard information, aligning with "engage in discussions about personal and school experiences based on texts heard" by simulating an informative discussion.</p>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on listening comprehension and participation, and a short written response for specific information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays one final short (approx. 45 seconds) audio narrative describing a lesser-known Panamanian animal (e.g., a tapir) and its habitat. Students listen and individually answer 3 specific comprehension questions in writing on an exit ticket.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What animal is described in the audio?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Where does this animal live (its habitat)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Mention one thing this animal does (a habit).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation when responding orally in group discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of target vocabulary from audio (e.g., <i>habitat, nocturnal, species</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in answering comprehension questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and participation in collaborative activities (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can identify specific information about animal habitats when I listen." (Yes / Mostly / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing I learned about Panama's wildlife today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the animal descriptions was the easiest for me? What was the most difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I practice my listening skills at home?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The use of varied audio inputs (short descriptions, narratives, sound quizzes) was effective for engaging students in listening tasks. The visuals paired with initial vocabulary modeling helped anchor meaning for A2.2 learners.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with differentiating between 'predator' and 'prey' even after clarification, indicating a need for more examples or a visual aid. I should also ensure audio speed is consistently appropriate for their level.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments for next lesson (Reading Comprehension): I will pre-teach these challenging vocabulary items again. I will ensure the reading text for Lesson 2 reinforces the same core vocabulary and grammatical structures introduced today, specifically focusing on comparative/superlative adjectives and descriptions of habitats using "there is/are" and present simple.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson, we will continue exploring Panama's wildlife by reading a text about these animals and their amazing habitats. We will use the vocabulary we learned today to understand the text better."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>